WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.580
<v Lecturer>So here is a lecture about how SSL works,</v>

2
00:00:02.580 --> 00:00:04.330
and how to protect yourself against

3
00:00:04.330 --> 00:00:05.780
Man-in-the-Middle attacks.

4
00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:07.190
Please do not skip this lecture

5
00:00:07.190 --> 00:00:08.520
even if you know SSL.

6
00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:09.810
I will go over the basics but trust me,

7
00:00:09.810 --> 00:00:11.590
there'll be some new content for you.

8
00:00:11.590 --> 00:00:15.090
So, SSL refers to Secure Socket Layer,

9
00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:17.260
and it's used to encrypt connections.

10
00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:19.730
And TLS refers to Transport Layer Security,

11
00:00:19.730 --> 00:00:22.100
which is a newer version of that protocol.

12
00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:26.880
And nowadays, even though it's almost only TLS certificates,

13
00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:30.090
people still refer, including me, of it as SSL.

14
00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:31.650
So if you hear me say SSL,

15
00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:34.040
I meant TLS, and it's just everyone does that.

16
00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:36.070
So don't get mad at me.

17
00:00:36.070 --> 00:00:38.080
So public SSL certificates are issued

18
00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:40.650
by something called the Certificate Authority.

19
00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:43.330
And you have many public Certificate Authorities,

20
00:00:43.330 --> 00:00:45.719
such as Comodo, Symantec, GoDaddy, GlobalSign,

21
00:00:45.719 --> 00:00:48.000
Digicert, Letsencrypt, and so on.

22
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:50.880
And when a certificate is issued to you,

23
00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:53.110
it has an expiration date that you will set,

24
00:00:53.110 --> 00:00:54.380
and then you must renew it

25
00:00:54.380 --> 00:00:57.590
before it expires to keep your service up and running.

26
00:00:57.590 --> 00:01:00.210
So, how does SSL encryption work?

27
00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:01.550
Well, you have a client and a server

28
00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:05.230
and they need to talk to one another using a secure channel.

29
00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:07.400
So they need to use SSL encryption.

30
00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:09.670
The thing is, using asymmetric encryption,

31
00:01:09.670 --> 00:01:13.180
which is the basis of SSL, is very, very expensive.

32
00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:16.810
And by expensive, we mean expensive in terms of CPU cycles.

33
00:01:16.810 --> 00:01:18.900
So the least expensive type

34
00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:22.110
of encryption is symmetric encryption, it's much cheaper.

35
00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:24.600
But SSL is only for asymmetric.

36
00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:27.120
So the idea is that during the asymmetric handshake,

37
00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:28.360
which is the first connection

38
00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:30.080
between the client and the server,

39
00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:33.610
they're going to use an exchange, a symmetric key

40
00:01:33.610 --> 00:01:36.600
that it will commonly share for this communication.

41
00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:37.920
And from that point forward,

42
00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:41.250
they will only use the symmetric key to talk to one another.

43
00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:43.690
So in this case, the asymmetric encryption of SSL

44
00:01:43.690 --> 00:01:45.360
is just used for the handshake,

45
00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:47.010
and then it's all symmetrical.

46
00:01:47.010 --> 00:01:48.770
So, how does that work in detail?

47
00:01:48.770 --> 00:01:51.050
The client says, hello, here is my cipher suits

48
00:01:51.050 --> 00:01:52.670
and here is a random thing.

49
00:01:52.670 --> 00:01:55.810
And the server responds, okay, here is my server random,

50
00:01:55.810 --> 00:01:57.930
and here is my SSL certificate.

51
00:01:57.930 --> 00:01:59.910
The client will verify the authenticity

52
00:01:59.910 --> 00:02:01.900
of the SSL certificate by looking

53
00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:04.380
up if it's correctly computed.

54
00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:05.600
And then if it is,

55
00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:08.490
it will send a master symmetric key that has been generated

56
00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:10.970
and sent, and they will be encrypted using

57
00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:12.530
the key that it has received.

58
00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:13.670
And the idea here is that,

59
00:02:13.670 --> 00:02:15.440
this secret will be able to be retrieved

60
00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:17.500
by the server and decrypted.

61
00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:20.140
And in case the client also has an SSL certificate,

62
00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:21.970
it will send it at this moment.

63
00:02:21.970 --> 00:02:24.440
So it's possible to have a two-way certificate

64
00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:26.580
where the client will find it gets to the server,

65
00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:28.730
and the server to the client.

66
00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:31.130
And then using the master key of the server,

67
00:02:31.130 --> 00:02:33.090
the private, using the private key,

68
00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:35.550
the master symmetric generated key

69
00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:36.710
by the client will be decrypted.

70
00:02:36.710 --> 00:02:39.000
And we will have a secure connection.

71
00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:40.640
That is going to be symmetrical this time,

72
00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:42.950
in place between the client and the server.

73
00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:44.560
You don't need to know all these details.

74
00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:46.610
The exam will not test you on this, but I want to

75
00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:48.350
make sure you understand how these handshake works,

76
00:02:48.350 --> 00:02:49.670
at least at a high level.

77
00:02:49.670 --> 00:02:52.660
So you get an idea of how SSL works, just for your knowledge

78
00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:54.430
because I think as a Solution Architect Professional,

79
00:02:54.430 --> 00:02:56.010
if you don't know how SSL works,

80
00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:57.590
that's a bit worrying, okay?

81
00:02:57.590 --> 00:02:58.990
But the exam will not test you

82
00:02:58.990 --> 00:03:01.890
on this exact, this exact knowledge.

83
00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:04.980
Definitely not, but, something it may test you on is,

84
00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:08.050
SSL, SNI, for Server Name Indication.

85
00:03:08.050 --> 00:03:10.440
So the idea is that SNI, will solve the problem

86
00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:12.300
of loading multiple SSL certificates

87
00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:14.490
onto one web server only.

88
00:03:14.490 --> 00:03:16.460
And that will allow you to serve multiple websites

89
00:03:16.460 --> 00:03:18.040
from that one web server.

90
00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:19.700
It's a newer kind of protocol.

91
00:03:19.700 --> 00:03:22.380
And so it requires the clients to indicate which

92
00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:23.960
host name it's trying to connect to,

93
00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:26.920
during this initial SSL handshake that we solved.

94
00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:29.960
And then the server will find the correct certificate,

95
00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:32.900
or it will return a default one if it doesn't find one.

96
00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:35.960
And so this works for the ALB and the NLB,

97
00:03:35.960 --> 00:03:38.310
which are the newer generation load balancers,

98
00:03:38.310 --> 00:03:39.300
and CloudFront.

99
00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:41.140
It does not work for your Classic Load Balancer,

100
00:03:41.140 --> 00:03:42.380
which is older gen.

101
00:03:42.380 --> 00:03:44.010
Very important to note.

102
00:03:44.010 --> 00:03:45.600
So we have our ALB,

103
00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:48.460
and it needs to be connected to two target group,

104
00:03:48.460 --> 00:03:50.560
which are connected to different host names.

105
00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:52.930
The first one is www.mycorp.com.

106
00:03:52.930 --> 00:03:56.470
And the second one is Domain1.example.com.

107
00:03:56.470 --> 00:03:59.610
So we're going to load two SSL certificates onto our ALB,

108
00:03:59.610 --> 00:04:00.570
but it could be more.

109
00:04:00.570 --> 00:04:02.110
And so one SSL certificate

110
00:04:02.110 --> 00:04:03.960
will correspond to the first target group,

111
00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:06.230
and the other one to the second target group.

112
00:04:06.230 --> 00:04:07.140
The idea now is that,

113
00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:10.870
a client who has SNI compatibility says, hey,

114
00:04:10.870 --> 00:04:13.173
I would like to connect to www.mycorp.com.

115
00:04:15.012 --> 00:04:17.562
And the ALB, because it has SNI enabled,

116
00:04:17.562 --> 00:04:20.810
will use the SSL, the correct SSL certificate,

117
00:04:20.810 --> 00:04:23.540
and then it will redirect it to the correct target group.

118
00:04:23.540 --> 00:04:25.600
And we'll have an encrypted connection.

119
00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:28.140
But in case we ask for obviously another domain,

120
00:04:28.140 --> 00:04:30.240
then the ALB will be smart enough to get the other

121
00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:33.210
SSL certificate and connect to the right target group.

122
00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:34.490
So SNI is extremely important.

123
00:04:34.490 --> 00:04:37.250
As soon as you have multiple SSL certificates.

124
00:04:37.250 --> 00:04:39.580
If you have a CLB, it doesn't work.

125
00:04:39.580 --> 00:04:42.230
So Classic Load Balancer, and it doesn't work with SNI.

126
00:04:42.230 --> 00:04:45.730
Then we cannot use one CLB behind multiple applications.

127
00:04:45.730 --> 00:04:48.572
You would need to have multiple CLB for multiple target,

128
00:04:48.572 --> 00:04:53.090
for multiple ASG, in order to get this architecture working.

129
00:04:53.090 --> 00:04:54.380
Okay.

130
00:04:54.380 --> 00:04:57.120
And next, extremely important, how to protect yourself

131
00:04:57.120 --> 00:04:59.540
against SSL Man-in-the-Middle attacks.

132
00:04:59.540 --> 00:05:01.080
So, how does it look like?

133
00:05:01.080 --> 00:05:03.050
We have the Good Server we're trying to reach,

134
00:05:03.050 --> 00:05:04.930
and our user is trying to talk to it,

135
00:05:04.930 --> 00:05:07.170
but there's a Pirate Server in between.

136
00:05:07.170 --> 00:05:09.220
And because it's HTTP, right now,

137
00:05:09.220 --> 00:05:11.620
it can intercept the packets and change them at will.

138
00:05:11.620 --> 00:05:12.550
And so it's in the middle

139
00:05:12.550 --> 00:05:14.380
and it can see the secrets and so on.

140
00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:15.640
It's really, really bad.

141
00:05:15.640 --> 00:05:17.130
So this is never happening

142
00:05:17.130 --> 00:05:20.930
on the web because there's almost no more HTTP available.

143
00:05:20.930 --> 00:05:22.160
But if you do have HTTP on the web,

144
00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:24.700
be very careful because you are public,

145
00:05:24.700 --> 00:05:27.240
you can be a target of Man-in-the-Middle attacks.

146
00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:29.650
So now the good way is to use HTTPS.

147
00:05:29.650 --> 00:05:32.090
So we have the same kind of architecture,

148
00:05:32.090 --> 00:05:33.700
but this time the Pirate Server will try to

149
00:05:33.700 --> 00:05:36.650
send a fake SSL certificate to the user.

150
00:05:36.650 --> 00:05:38.700
By decrypting the packets from the Good Server,

151
00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:40.930
and re encrypting them, and maybe changing them.

152
00:05:40.930 --> 00:05:44.080
And so if the user is not infected, it's going to detect

153
00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:45.790
it's a fake SSL certificate,

154
00:05:45.790 --> 00:05:47.990
and not react to it and just say, okay,

155
00:05:47.990 --> 00:05:49.960
I'm not communicating with that Pirate Server.

156
00:05:49.960 --> 00:05:52.460
But in case the Pirate Server did manage to get

157
00:05:52.460 --> 00:05:53.950
onto the user's machine,

158
00:05:53.950 --> 00:05:58.370
and make the pirate certificate trustable by the user.

159
00:05:58.370 --> 00:05:59.220
Then the user will say, oh,

160
00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:01.630
this looks like, it's a certificate I trust.

161
00:06:01.630 --> 00:06:04.770
And the Pirate Server has successfully done a

162
00:06:04.770 --> 00:06:06.750
Man-in-the-Middle attack.

163
00:06:06.750 --> 00:06:09.800
So how do we prevent a Man-in-the-Middle attack?

164
00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:10.633
Number one is,

165
00:06:10.633 --> 00:06:14.240
do not use public-facing HTTP servers, use HTTPS.

166
00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:16.820
That means that you leverage SSL and TLS certificates.

167
00:06:16.820 --> 00:06:19.320
And that means that the content is encrypted

168
00:06:19.320 --> 00:06:22.260
between your clients and your servers.

169
00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:24.460
That doesn't prevent the second vector of attack which is,

170
00:06:24.460 --> 00:06:26.550
to have a DNS attack.

171
00:06:26.550 --> 00:06:29.660
So if you're using a DNS, that has DNSSEC enabled,

172
00:06:30.801 --> 00:06:33.160
that means that, the DNS is going to be protected

173
00:06:33.160 --> 00:06:34.320
against the type of attacks as well.

174
00:06:34.320 --> 00:06:35.153
Why?

175
00:06:35.153 --> 00:06:37.300
Well, when a DNS server is hacked,

176
00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:40.930
or is possible for a pirate server to forge a DNS response,

177
00:06:40.930 --> 00:06:45.090
and to send your clients to the wrong target server.

178
00:06:45.090 --> 00:06:46.500
But you can protect against that

179
00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:50.890
by having your domain name configured with DNSSEC.

180
00:06:50.890 --> 00:06:55.740
So Amazon Route 53 supports DNSSEC for domain registration.

181
00:06:55.740 --> 00:06:58.030
And, as of December 2020,

182
00:06:58.030 --> 00:07:00.630
it also supports DNS security, DNSSEC,

183
00:07:00.630 --> 00:07:03.400
for DNS service itself using KMS.

184
00:07:03.400 --> 00:07:07.010
So both domain registration, and the DNS service,

185
00:07:07.010 --> 00:07:10.920
are now DNSSEC compliant on Route 53.

186
00:07:10.920 --> 00:07:14.670
You could alternatively run a custom DNS server on EC2,

187
00:07:14.670 --> 00:07:15.503
for example.

188
00:07:15.503 --> 00:07:17.360
One of them could be Bind, which is the most popular,

189
00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:19.381
but you have other ones which is dnsmasq,

190
00:07:19.381 --> 00:07:21.260
KnotDNS, or PowerDNS.

191
00:07:21.260 --> 00:07:23.530
And configure DNSSEC directly

192
00:07:23.530 --> 00:07:25.270
on your custom institute instance.

193
00:07:25.270 --> 00:07:26.960
But right now, there's a change,

194
00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:30.590
and Amazon Route 53 supports DNSSEC out of the box,

195
00:07:30.590 --> 00:07:33.850
as of December 2020, that used to not be the case.

196
00:07:33.850 --> 00:07:35.230
All right, that's it for this lecture.

197
00:07:35.230 --> 00:07:36.063
I hope you liked it.

198
00:07:36.063 --> 00:07:37.930
And I will see you in the next lecture.

